ated technology, quick correct decisions; D solves problems extremely well, concentration.\n\nCalmness, judgment, performance under pressure: B handles stress well; C keeps calm in crisis; D responds adequately to unexpected events; A? no info; D's hot temper/moody may affect.\n\nDecision-making: C quick correct decisions; B? no info; A? anticipates dangerous situations (maybe proactive); D solves problems extremely well (good decision). B unknown.\n\nTeamwork, communication: A has very good leadership but also regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas; no explicit teamwork. B makes nasty remarks, can be uncooperative, grumpy; negative. C difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric; negative. D loner, moody, know-it-all; negative.\n\nProfessional development/long-term suitability: B has weak memory for numbers (maybe training), but no info on education; C not willing to further education (negative). A not open to new ideas (negative). D? no info.\n\nThus tradeoffs: B strong on stress, weather, conscientiousness, but interpersonal issues (nasty remarks, uncooperative). D strong problem solving, responsibility, but moody, loner, know-it-all, no teamwork. C strong decision speed, calm, technical, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education. A strong anticipation, spatial, leadership, but show-off, not open to new ideas, no stress info.\n\nWhich candidate best balances? Long-distance flights require crew coordination, calm under pressure, decision-making, technical competence, reliability. Interpersonal issues can be critical. B's nasty remarks and uncooperative may be severe. D's loner/moody also severe. C's communication difficulty and egocentric also severe. A's show-off and not open to new ideas may affect crew but perhaps less severe than outright hostility? Also A's leadership could be beneficial. Lack of stress handling info is a gap, but maybe his anticipation and leadership imply reliability.\n\nGiven the tradeoffs, I might lean toward Candidate A as provisional, because technical strengths and leadership outweigh interpersonal negatives, which may be mitigated. However, lack of stress handling is uncertain. But B's interpersonal negativity seems more severe for crew safety. D's moodiness also severe. C's communication difficulty also severe.\n\nThus I will provisionally prefer Candidate A, with moderate confidence, pending info on stress handling and teamwork. Uncertainty: if A's lack of openness to new ideas leads to poor adaptation, or if stress handling is poor, could shift.\n\nNow produce public message."}
